# Viral Hepatitis

- **Viral hepatitis** - infection of the liver by hepatotrophic viruses, important DDx for anyone presenting with elevated hepatocellular enzymes
- **Etiological agents of viral hepatitis:**
  - Hepatitis A-E
  - Cytomegalovirus, Epstein-Barr virus, Herpes seimplex, yellow fever
  - \
  ![\](<https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/firescript-577a2.appspot.com/o/imgs%2Fapp%2FJethro%2FvYwLM-UHll.png?alt=media&token=b8ee608a-06d4-433b-afd0-755112e61c1e>)
- **Clinical features of acute viral hepatitis** - same principles for acute viral hepatitis, or acute exacerbation of chronic viral hepatitis:
  - **Assymptomatic/ anicteric** - a large proportions of infected individuals have no symptoms, especially in young subjects
  - **Pre-icteric phase** - non-specific prodromal illness prior to onset of jaundice by few days to 2 weeks:
    - Low grade fever - typically \< 39 degrees
    - Severe fatigue, headaches, arthralgia, myalgia and anorexia
    - N/V - emetic centre stimulated by information from the hepatobilliary tract
    - RUQ dull ache - due to liver capsule distension (abundant nervous supply)
    - Features of intra-hepatic cholestasis (e.g. tea coloured urine, pale stools) - due to swelling of hepatocytes
  - **Icteric phase** - jaundice
  - **Convalescent phase** - resolution within 3-6 weeks
- **Signs of acute hepatitis** - typically few:
  - RUQ tenderness
  - Mild hepatomegaly
  - Splenomegaly (ocassionally)
  - Cervical lymphadenopathy (ocassionally)
- **Ix of acute hepatitis:**
  - **CBC** - reactive lymphocytosis
  - **LFT** - hepatic pattern of LFT develop:
    - Elevation of hepatocellular enzymes - degree of elevation in the thousands (degree of elevation reflects amount of hepatocyte damage)
    - Fulminant hepatitis –\> manifest as falling AST and ALT
    - Hyperbilirubinaemia (direct mainly) - reflects intra-hepatic cholestasis and can remain elevated after clinical recovery
  - **PT** - reflects severity and prognostication of hepatitis
  - **Serological tests** - confirm etiology of infection
- **Mx of acute hepatitis:**
  - Hospital care not needed
  - **Diet** - no specific dietary modifications (D5 does not help)
  - **Alcohol** - avoided for 6 mo after acute hepatitis episode, lifelong for chronic hepatitis
  - **Drugs** - avoid drugs requiring liver metabolism, no known drugs hasten course of hepatitis

## Hepatitis A

- **Virology of hepatitis A** - Picornavirus (RNA virus)
- **Epidemiology:**
  - **Incidence** - Reducing incidence over last decade, but mean age of infection increasing (worrying as mortality increased)
  - **Mortality a/w age:**
    - \< 14y - 0.1%
    - 15-39y - 0.4%
    - \>= 40y - 1.1%
- **Transmission of hepatitis A:**
  - Faecal-oral route (common):
    - Faecal route - viral shedding during incubation (2-3 weeks) up until 7-10d after onset of icteric phase (children, sex, and rarely healthcare workers)
    - Oral route - ingestion of contaminated shellfish (not well cooked) or infected water
  - Parenteral route (rare) - transfusion, acupuncture, tattoos
- **Clinical course of hepatitis A:**
  - Self-limiting course of viral hepatitis - more symptomatic in adults compared w/ children:
    - Children - 70% anicteric or mildly symptomatic
    - Adults - \> 70% icteric hepatitis
  - Diarrhoea is comparatively common in early stages of disease
  - Complete recovery occurs within 4-6 weeks
- **Complications of hepatitis A:**
  - **Cholestatic hepatitis** - prolonged disease course (12-24 w) with marked jaundice and pruritis, but always complete resolution:
    - LFT - hyperbilirubinaemia (x 10 ULN) but with decreasing transaminase
    - Histopathology - due to centrilobular cholestasis w/ periportal inflammation
    - Mx - steroid whitewashes disease but a/w increased relapse risk (not used)
  - **Relapse** (6-10%) - biphasic or polyphasic biochemical and clinical relapse due to immune reactions
    - Ix - increased transaminase (\> 1000 IU/L), persistent +ve IgM Anti-HAV, HAV RNA in serum, HAV shedding in stool
  - **Extrahepatic manifestations** (\< 15%) - due to immune complex diseases:
    - Purpuric rash
    - Arthralgia
    - Cryoglobulinaemia
  - **Trigger for autoimmune hepatitis** (in predisposed individuals)
- **Diagnosis of hepatitis A:**
  - **IgM anti-HAV** - detected 1-2 weeks after jaundice, or 3-4 weeks after AST/ALT
    - **Short-lived** - persists for 3-4 months, 25% for 6 mo, rarely for 12 mo
  - IgG anti-HAV - not diagnostic for active infection, indicates immunity against HAV
- **Prevention of hepatitis A:**
  - **General measures:**
    - Careful cooking of shelled seafood:
      - Dry heat at 100 degrees in 1 min
      - Wet heat at 100 degrees in 5-10 minutes
      - 60 degrees heat 12 h still not killed
    - Chlorination of drinking water
  - **Passive immunization** - up to 90% efficacy, but dose-dependent and \< 6 months protection
  - **Vaccination** - Inactivated whole virus from cell cultures (2 IMI injections 6 mo apart)
    - **Indications** - Chronic HBV/ HCV carriers, non-immune adult travellers, close contacts
    - **Efficacy** - Effective protection within 3-4 weeks after 1st dose (99% immunogenicity, 100% protective efficacy, unknown duration of protection)

## Hepatitis E

- **Virology** - Calcivirus (RNA)
  - Genotype 1 and 2 - human reservoir
  - Genotype 3 and 4 - zoonotic reservoir (swine, rats)
- **Epidemiology:**
  - **Incidence** - increasing incidence, more prevalent than HAV, acounts for 33.6% of hepatitis in HK (2016 data)
  - **Higher mortality rate** - 1-2% in normal patients (cf 0.4% in HAV), up to 20% in pregnant women
- **Transmission** - faecal-oral route (through waterbourne transmission or zoonotic faeces)
- **Clinical features of Hepatitis E:**
  - Clinical course - clinically identical to HAV, but no permanent protection after infection
- **Diagnosis of Hepatitis E:**
  - IgM Anti-HEV - coincides with symptoms and persists for 3 mo
  - IgG Anti-HEV - follows rise of IgM, persists for several years (not life-long)
  - PCR assay for HEV
